    "id": "chapter-16",
    "number": 16,
    "title": "Chapter 8 — Common Mistakes Across All Three Models: The 8 Traps to Avoid",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Chapter 8 — Common Mistakes Across All Three Models\nMistake 1: Treating AI output as a finished product\nAI can generate text, images, and store pages quickly. It cannot guarantee accuracy, usefulness, trust, compliance, or demand.",
      "Correction: review every customer-facing asset and test the offer with real people.\nMistake 2: Choosing a business because it looks easy\nA quick demo hides months of publishing, supplier management, customer support, or distribution work.",
      "Correction: evaluate the ongoing operating burden, not just the setup.\nMistake 3: Confusing revenue with profit\nSubscription payments are not pure profit. Digital-product sales still incur fees and acquisition costs. Affiliate commissions can be reversed or withheld under programme terms.",
      "Correction: calculate contribution after variable costs and monitor cash flow.\nMistake 4: Building before validating\nA finished store or polished guide is not proof of demand.",
      "Correction: test the problem and offer with the smallest credible prototype.\nMistake 5: Competing only on price\nA lower price is easy for a competitor to copy and may leave no room for acquisition or service costs.",
      "Correction: create a meaningful difference in usefulness, convenience, focus, experience, or customer fit.\nMistake 6: Relying on one platform\nAn account, algorithm, marketplace, or affiliate programme can change its rules.",
      "Correction: preserve your content, maintain customer records lawfully, and develop more than one route to reach your audience.\nMistake 7: Assuming recurring revenue is automatic\nSubscriptions require retention. Memberships require ongoing value. A customer does not owe you another payment simply because you built a recurring billing option.",
      "Correction: make the continuing value obvious and make cancellation clear.\nMistake 8: Using misleading AI-generated claims\nSynthetic testimonials, fake credentials, unrealistic product images, and unsupported performance claims can destroy trust and create legal or platform risk.",
      "Correction: disclose synthetic characters where needed, disclose affiliate relationships, and use substantiated product claims."
    ]
